\chapter{Course links}
\label{sec:course-links}

Content in Canvas links to other content in the same course: a page links
to the grading criteria, an assignment links to the lecture slides, a quiz
question links to a file.
Canvas writes those links with the course's numeric id in them, so a body
copied into another course keeps pointing at the course it came from.
This chapter gives every content command one translation layer: on the way
out of Canvas the links are rewritten into a course-independent symbolic
form, and on the way in the symbols are resolved against the course the
content is pushed into.

\section{The problem: content that names its course}

The Rich Content Editor writes a link to a page like this:
\begin{verbatim}
<a href="https://canvas.kth.se/courses/59543/pages/grading-criteria"
   data-api-endpoint="https://canvas.kth.se/api/v1/courses/59543/pages/grading-criteria"
   data-api-returntype="Page" title="Grading criteria">grading criteria</a>
\end{verbatim}
Two attributes name the course.
The [[href]] is what the browser follows; sometimes it is absolute as
above, sometimes relative ([[/courses/59543/pages/grading-criteria]]).
The [[data-api-endpoint]] is a courtesy to API consumers: it points at the
API representation of the same object, and [[data-api-returntype]] says
what type comes back.
Links to assignments, files, modules and discussions have the same shape
with a numeric id instead of the page slug, and images use [[src]] instead
of [[href]].

The workflow this project supports is to pull a course's pages,
assignments and quizzes into Markdown files under version control
([[pages view]], [[assignments view]], \ldots) and push them into next
year's course instance ([[pages edit -f]], \ldots).
A file pulled from course 59543 and pushed into course 71234 keeps every
[[/courses/59543/]] link, so students in the new course are sent to a
course they cannot see.
The [[data-api-endpoint]] attributes go stale the same way.

\section{How Canvas solves it, and why that is not enough}

Canvas's own course copy has the same problem and solves it in two steps.
The exporter rewrites every link that points into the exported course
into a placeholder:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[$CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE$/<path>]] ] the course itself, for any
  path under it;
\item[ [[$WIKI_REFERENCE$/pages/<slug>]] ] a page, identified by its URL
  slug;
\item[ [[$CANVAS_OBJECT_REFERENCE$/<type>/<migration id>]] ] an assignment,
  quiz, discussion or module, identified by a migration id;
\item[ [[$IMS-CC-FILEBASE$/<folder>/<file>]] ] a file, identified by its
  path below the course files root.
\end{description}
The importer then resolves them in the destination course: pages by slug,
files by path, and the rest through the migration ids it assigned while
copying.
The [[data-api-endpoint]] attributes are left alone, so they are stale in
every copied course.

We cannot simply write those placeholders and let Canvas do the rest.
They are interpreted only by the content-migration importer; the REST API
that this tool uses to create and update content stores them as the
literal, broken URLs they look like.
And the migration ids that identify everything except pages and files are
internal to the importer; the API never exposes them.
So the resolution has to happen in this tool, and objects have to be
identified by something the API does give us: the page's slug, the file's
path, and for everything else its name.

\section{A symbolic form of our own}
\label{sec:links-syntax}

We keep Canvas's placeholders as the outer syntax, so that anyone who has
looked inside a course export recognises the links, and put names where
Canvas puts migration ids.
A course URL is rewritten as follows.
\begin{description}
\item[ [[/pages/<slug>]] ] becomes [[$WIKI_REFERENCE$/pages/<slug>]], the
  form Canvas itself uses; the slug is course-independent already.
\item[ [[/assignments/<id>]] ] becomes
  [[$CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE$/assignments/<name>]].
  New Quizzes are assignments and appear under this path too.
\item[ [[/quizzes/<id>]], [[/discussion_topics/<id>]], [[/modules/<id>]] ]
  likewise become the type followed by the quiz title, the topic title or
  the module name.
  Announcements are discussion topics.
\item[ [[/modules/items/<id>]] ] becomes
  [[$CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE$/modules/items/<module name>/<item title>]],
  since an item title is only unique within its module.
\item[ [[/files/<id>/download?wrap=1]] ] becomes
  [[$IMS-CC-FILEBASE$/<folder>/<file>?canvas_download=1&wrap=1]]: the file
  is named by its path below the course files root, as in a Canvas export,
  and the verb that followed the id ([[download]], [[preview]],
  [[inline_view]]) moves into a query parameter so that a folder or file
  named [[download]] cannot be mistaken for it.
\item[any other path] becomes [[$CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE$/<path>]]
  verbatim.
  This covers the list pages ([[/assignments]], [[/discussion_topics]],
  [[/announcements]], [[/modules]], [[/grades]], [[/files]]), the syllabus
  ([[/assignments/syllabus]]), folder views ([[/files/folder/<path>]]) and
  the course front page.
\item[an id that no longer exists] becomes
  [[$CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE$/<type>/id:<id>]] and is reported.
  The number is kept so that nothing is lost, and the [[id:]] prefix keeps
  it from being mistaken for a name.
\end{description}

\subsection{Paths versus names}

The same prefix, [[$CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE$/assignments]], starts both
the assignment list and a link to one assignment, so the reader of a symbol
must know which segments are literal and which are names.
The rule is by position: the first segment after the token is the type and
is literal; a second segment under a type that has named objects is a
name, except for the reserved literals Canvas uses there
([[/assignments/syllabus]], [[/files/folder]]); and [[/modules/items]]
takes two names.
A path that fits none of these is literal throughout.
This is the same rule Canvas's routes follow, which is why the numeric
URLs can be parsed the same way in the other direction.

\subsection{Encoding names into URLs}

Names contain spaces, slashes, quotes and non-ASCII letters, none of which
can appear raw inside a URL in an HTML attribute.
We percent-encode a name with nothing marked safe, so that a slash inside
a name becomes [[%2F]] and a symbol still splits unambiguously on the
slashes that separate its segments, and a quote becomes [[%22]] and can
never end the attribute early.
Pandoc leaves link destinations alone in both directions
(\cref{sec:html-to-markdown}), so an encoded name survives the
Markdown round trip unchanged.

\subsection{Tokens accepted but not produced}

Resolution accepts every form Canvas's exporter writes, so that content
lifted out of an export or written by hand in the Canvas spelling works
too: [[$CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE$/pages/<slug>]] is taken as a page, and
[[$WIKI_REFERENCE$/wiki/<slug>]] is Canvas's older spelling of the same.
The one form we cannot honour is
[[$CANVAS_OBJECT_REFERENCE$/<type>/<migration id>]]: without the
importer's tables a migration id names nothing, so such a link is
reported as unresolvable rather than guessed at.

\section{Module outline}

The module is a library for the content commands, not a subcommand of its
own.
It depends on [[canvaslms.cli]] for the diagnostics helpers and on nothing
else in the package: [[content.nw]] imports it, and the content modules
import [[content.nw]], so a dependency back would be a cycle.
For the same reason it talks to the [[canvasapi]] course object directly
rather than through the filter functions of [[pages.nw]] or
[[assignments.nw]]; those objects go through the caches described
later in this chapter anyway.
