\section{From a course to a symbol}
\label{sec:course-links-symbolize}

Exporting a body means visiting every link attribute, keeping every link
that does not point into this course, and rewriting the ones that do.
The course id in the URL must be this course's: a link to another course
on the same server is a cross-course link the author made on purpose,
and Canvas's own exporter leaves those alone too.

What could go wrong is recorded rather than raised.
An export must never fail because a link is stale---the user wants the
file, and a stale link is exactly what they will find and fix in
it---so the function returns the rewritten body together with a list of
[[LinkProblem]]s for the caller to report.
<<classes>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass
class LinkProblem:
  """A link that could not be translated, and why.

  `kind` is `missing` (no such object), `ambiguous` (several objects
  of that name) or `unsupported` (a form we cannot translate).
  """
  url: str
  kind: str
  reason: str
<<functions>>=
def symbolize_links(body, course, server_url=None):
  """Rewrite the links into `course` in `body` to symbolic form.

  Returns `(body, problems)`: the body with every link into `course`
  replaced by its symbol (see the chapter on course links in the
  manual), and a list of `LinkProblem` for links whose target no
  longer exists or whose name is not unique. Links to other courses,
  other sites and already symbolic links are left untouched.
  `server_url` is the base URL of the Canvas server, needed to
  recognise absolute links; without it only relative links are
  translated.
  """
  index = CourseIndex(course)
  problems = []

  def rewrite(attribute, url):
    link = parse_course_url(url, server_url)
    if link is None or link.course_id != course.id:
      return None
    symbol, extra = symbolize_path(link.path, index, problems)
    query = drop_query_params(link.query, VOLATILE_PARAMS)
    return symbol + add_query_params(query, extra) + link.fragment

  return rewrite_link_attributes(body, rewrite), problems
@

\subsection{One mapping per object type}

The path under the course decides the symbol, following the rules of
\cref{sec:links-syntax}.
Module items are matched before the generic object pattern, because
[[/modules/items/345]] would otherwise parse as a module named
[[items]].
<<constants>>=
MODULE_ITEM_PATH = re.compile(r'^/modules/items/(?P<id>\d+)$')
OBJECT_PATH = re.compile(
  r'^/(?P<type>pages|assignments|quizzes|discussion_topics|modules|files)'
  r'/(?P<id>[^/]+)(?P<tail>/.*)?$')
FILE_VERB = re.compile(r'^/(?P<verb>download|preview|inline_view)$')
<<functions>>=
def symbolize_path(path, index, problems):
  """Return `(symbol, extra query parameters)` for the course path `path`.

  `problems` collects a `LinkProblem` for an id that names nothing or
  a name that is not unique.
  """
  match = MODULE_ITEM_PATH.match(path)
  if match:
    return symbolize_module_item(int(match['id']), index, problems), []
  match = OBJECT_PATH.match(path)
  if match:
    kind, ident, tail = match['type'], match['id'], match['tail'] or ""
    if kind == "pages":
      return f"{WIKI_TOKEN}/pages/{ident}{tail}", []
    if ident.isdigit():
      if kind == "files":
        return symbolize_file(int(ident), tail, index, problems)
      return symbolize_named(kind, int(ident), tail, index, problems), []
  return COURSE_TOKEN + path, []
@

An object named by its name is the common case.
When the name is shared with another object of the same kind the symbol
is still written, since the name is the best identity we have, but the
problem is reported so that the user learns about the clash a year
before it makes the import refuse the link.
<<functions>>=
def symbolize_named(kind, object_id, tail, index, problems):
  """Return the symbol for the object of `kind` with `object_id`."""
  name = index.name(kind, object_id)
  if name is None:
    problems.append(LinkProblem(
      f"/{kind}/{object_id}", "missing",
      f"no {kind_label(kind)} with id {object_id} in the course"))
    return f"{COURSE_TOKEN}/{kind}/id:{object_id}{tail}"
  if index.is_ambiguous(kind, name):
    problems.append(LinkProblem(
      f"/{kind}/{object_id}", "ambiguous",
      f"the name {name!r} is shared by several {kind_label(kind, 2)}"))
  return f"{COURSE_TOKEN}/{kind}/{encode_name(name)}{tail}"

def symbolize_module_item(item_id, index, problems):
  """Return the symbol for the module item with `item_id`."""
  found = index.module_items().get(item_id)
  if found is None:
    problems.append(LinkProblem(
      f"/modules/items/{item_id}", "missing",
      f"no module item with id {item_id} in the course"))
    return f"{COURSE_TOKEN}/modules/items/id:{item_id}"
  module, item = found
  return (f"{COURSE_TOKEN}/modules/items/{encode_name(module.name)}"
          f"/{encode_name(item.title)}")
@

A file link may end in a verb ([[/download]], [[/preview]],
[[/inline_view]]) that says what the browser should do with the file.
The verb travels as a query parameter, [[canvas_download=1]] and so on,
in the spelling Canvas's own exports use for the download case.
<<functions>>=
def symbolize_file(file_id, tail, index, problems):
  """Return `(symbol, extra query parameters)` for the file `file_id`."""
  verb = FILE_VERB.match(tail)
  extra = [(f"canvas_{verb['verb']}", "1")] if verb else []
  tail = "" if verb else tail
  found = index.file_path(file_id)
  if found is None:
    problems.append(LinkProblem(
      f"/files/{file_id}", "missing",
      f"no file with id {file_id} in the course"))
    return f"{COURSE_TOKEN}/files/id:{file_id}{tail}", extra
  folder, name = found
  path = "/".join(encode_name(part) for part in folder.split("/") if part)
  return f"{FILE_TOKEN}/{path}/{encode_name(name)}".replace("//", "/") \
    + tail, extra
@

The names go through the encoding of \cref{sec:links-syntax}; the
labels in messages are the English words for the kinds.
<<constants>>=
KIND_LABELS = {
  "assignments": ("assignment", "assignments"),
  "quizzes": ("quiz", "quizzes"),
  "discussion_topics": ("discussion", "discussions"),
  "modules": ("module", "modules"),
  "pages": ("page", "pages"),
  "files": ("file", "files"),
}
<<functions>>=
def encode_name(name):
  """Percent-encode `name` so that it is one URL path segment."""
  return urllib.parse.quote(name, safe="")

def decode_name(segment):
  """Undo `encode_name`."""
  return urllib.parse.unquote(segment)

def kind_label(kind, count=1):
  """Return the English word for objects of `kind`, plural if `count > 1`."""
  singular, plural = KIND_LABELS[kind]
  return singular if count == 1 else plural

def add_query_params(query, params):
  """Prepend the `(key, value)` pairs in `params` to the query `query`."""
  if not params:
    return query
  existing = urllib.parse.parse_qsl(query[1:], keep_blank_values=True) \
    if query else []
  return "?" + urllib.parse.urlencode(list(params) + existing)
@

