\section{From a symbol to a course}
\label{sec:course-links-resolve}

Importing is the inverse: every symbolic link becomes a link into the
course the body is pushed into, and every other link is left alone.
A symbol that cannot be resolved is also left alone, in symbolic form,
and reported.
That choice is what makes pushing a whole course possible at all: the
first page pushed into an empty course links to pages that do not exist
yet, and a symbol that stays symbolic is resolved by the next push once
they do.
Whether an unresolved link stops the push is decided by the caller
(\cref{sec:course-links-report}); the function itself never guesses.
<<functions>>=
def resolve_links(body, course, server_url=None):
  """Rewrite the symbolic links in `body` into links into `course`.

  Returns `(body, problems)`: the body with every symbol replaced by a
  course URL, and a list of `LinkProblem` for symbols that name no
  object in `course`, several objects, or use a form we cannot
  resolve. Those symbols are left as they are. `server_url` is used
  for the absolute `data-api-endpoint` attribute and defaults to the
  server `course` came from.
  """
  index = CourseIndex(course)
  server_url = server_url or server_url_for(course)
  problems = []

  def rewrite(attribute, url):
    symbol = parse_symbolic_url(url)
    if symbol is None:
      return None
    resolved = resolve_symbol(url, symbol, index, problems)
    if resolved is None:
      return None
    path, query = resolved
    <<shape the resolved URL for its attribute>>

  return rewrite_link_attributes(body, rewrite), problems
@

\subsection{Why the API endpoint needs no separate repair}

Canvas's importer leaves [[data-api-endpoint]] stale because it treats
it as an attribute it does not know.
We symbolized it like any other link, so on the way back it is resolved
like any other link; the only difference is the shape of the result.
The editor writes [[href]] and [[src]] relative, which survives a change
of server, and [[data-api-endpoint]] absolute with the API prefix.
The attribute name is all that is needed to tell them apart, and
[[data-api-returntype]] names the object's type, which does not change,
so it is never touched.
<<shape the resolved URL for its attribute>>=
if attribute == "data-api-endpoint":
  base = f"{server_url or ''}/api/v1/courses/{course.id}"
else:
  base = f"/courses/{course.id}"
return base + path + query + symbol.fragment
@

The server URL comes from the requester the course object carries,
which is the base URL the user logged in with.
<<functions>>=
def server_url_for(course):
  """Return the base URL of the Canvas server `course` came from."""
  try:
    return course._requester.original_url.rstrip("/")
  except AttributeError:
    return None
@

\subsection{Matching order}

The token says what the path names, and within the course token the
same positional rule as on export applies, in reverse.
Every branch returns the path and query to put after the course, or
[[None]] after recording why.
<<constants>>=
PAGE_SYMBOL = re.compile(r'^/(?:pages|wiki)/(?P<slug>[^/]+)(?P<tail>/.*)?$')
MODULE_ITEM_SYMBOL = re.compile(
  r'^/modules/items/(?P<module>[^/]+)/(?P<title>[^/]+)$')
<<functions>>=
def resolve_symbol(url, symbol, index, problems):
  """Return `(path, query)` under the course for `symbol`, or None."""
  if symbol.token == OBJECT_TOKEN:
    problems.append(LinkProblem(
      url, "unsupported",
      "migration ids are not available through the API"))
    return None
  if symbol.token == FILE_TOKEN:
    return resolve_file(url, symbol, index, problems)
  if symbol.token == WIKI_TOKEN:
    match = PAGE_SYMBOL.match(symbol.path)
    if not match:
      problems.append(LinkProblem(url, "unsupported",
                                  "not a page reference"))
      return None
    return resolve_page(url, match['slug'], match['tail'] or "",
                        symbol.query, index, problems)
  <<resolve a course token path>>
@

Under the course token, module items come first for the same reason as
on export.
Then the object types with names; a name that starts with [[id:]] was
written because the object was already gone when the body was exported,
which is worth its own message.
Whatever is left is a literal path.
<<resolve a course token path>>=
match = MODULE_ITEM_SYMBOL.match(symbol.path)
if match:
  return resolve_module_item(url, decode_name(match['module']),
                             decode_name(match['title']),
                             symbol.query, index, problems)
match = OBJECT_PATH.match(symbol.path)
if match:
  kind, ident, tail = match['type'], match['id'], match['tail'] or ""
  if kind == "pages":
    return resolve_page(url, ident, tail, symbol.query, index, problems)
  if ident.startswith("id:"):
    problems.append(LinkProblem(
      url, "missing",
      f"the {kind_label(kind)} no longer existed when the link was "
      "exported"))
    return None
  if kind in NAMED_KINDS and ident != "syllabus":
    return resolve_named(url, kind, decode_name(ident), tail,
                         symbol.query, index, problems)
return symbol.path, symbol.query
@

The three lookups share one shape: ask the index for candidates, accept
exactly one, and report zero or several.
<<functions>>=
def resolve_named(url, kind, name, tail, query, index, problems):
  """Return the path of the object of `kind` named `name`, or None."""
  found = index.candidates(kind, name)
  if len(found) == 1:
    return f"/{kind}/{found[0].id}{tail}", query
  report_candidates(url, found, f"{kind_label(kind)} named {name!r}",
                    kind_label(kind, 2), problems)
  return None

def resolve_page(url, slug, tail, query, index, problems):
  """Return the path of the page reachable under `slug`, or None."""
  found = index.page_candidates(slug)
  if len(found) == 1:
    return f"/pages/{found[0].url}{tail}", query
  report_candidates(url, found, f"page with slug {slug!r}", "pages",
                    problems)
  return None

def resolve_module_item(url, module_name, title, query, index, problems):
  """Return the path of the item `title` in module `module_name`."""
  found = index.module_item_candidates(module_name, title)
  if len(found) == 1:
    return f"/modules/items/{found[0].id}", query
  report_candidates(url, found,
                    f"item {title!r} in module {module_name!r}",
                    "module items", problems)
  return None

def report_candidates(url, found, what, plural, problems):
  """Record why `found` is not exactly one object."""
  if not found:
    problems.append(LinkProblem(url, "missing",
                                f"no {what} in the course"))
  else:
    problems.append(LinkProblem(
      url, "ambiguous", f"{len(found)} {plural} match {what}"))
@

A file symbol carries its verb in the query; on resolution the verb goes
back after the id and its parameter disappears from the query.
<<constants>>=
FILE_VERB_PARAMS = ("canvas_download", "canvas_preview",
                    "canvas_inline_view")
<<functions>>=
def resolve_file(url, symbol, index, problems):
  """Return the path of the file at the path in `symbol`, or None."""
  segments = [decode_name(s) for s in symbol.path.split("/") if s]
  if not segments:
    problems.append(LinkProblem(url, "unsupported", "no file name"))
    return None
  folder, name = "/".join(segments[:-1]), segments[-1]
  found = index.file_candidates(folder, name)
  if len(found) != 1:
    report_candidates(url, found, f"file {symbol.path[1:]!r}", "files",
                      problems)
    return None
  params = dict(urllib.parse.parse_qsl(symbol.query[1:],
                                       keep_blank_values=True)) \
    if symbol.query else {}
  verbs = [p[len("canvas_"):] for p in FILE_VERB_PARAMS if p in params]
  tail = f"/{verbs[0]}" if verbs else ""
  return (f"/files/{found[0].id}{tail}",
          drop_query_params(symbol.query, FILE_VERB_PARAMS))
@

