\section{Reporting what could not be resolved}
\label{sec:course-links-report}

The problems come back as data so that the command decides what they
mean.
On export they are warnings: the file is what the user asked for, and
the stale link is in it for them to see.
On import the default is to stop before anything is written.
A link that stays symbolic in Canvas is visibly broken to students, and
a push that half succeeds is harder to notice than one that refuses;
the user who is bootstrapping a course and knows the targets come later
asks for the warning instead with [[--allow-unresolved-links]], and
pushes again once they exist.
<<functions>>=
def report_link_problems(problems, course, fatal):
  """Report `problems` for `course`; exit with status 1 if `fatal`."""
  if not problems:
    return
  seen = []
  for problem in problems:
    line = f"  {problem.url}: {problem.reason}"
    if line not in seen:
      seen.append(line)
  course_name = getattr(course, "name", None) or f"course {course.id}"
  message = (f"{len(seen)} link(s) could not be translated in "
             f"{course_name}:\n" + "\n".join(seen))
  if fatal:
    canvaslms.cli.err(1, message)
  canvaslms.cli.warn(message)
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_report_is_silent_without_problems(capsys):
  report_link_problems([], fake_course(), fatal=True)

def test_report_exits_when_fatal_and_warns_otherwise(caplog):
  problems = [LinkProblem("$WIKI_REFERENCE$/pages/x", "missing", "no page"),
              LinkProblem("$WIKI_REFERENCE$/pages/x", "missing", "no page")]
  with pytest.raises(SystemExit) as exit_info:
    report_link_problems(problems, fake_course(), fatal=True)
  assert exit_info.value.code == 1
  caplog.clear()
  with caplog.at_level("WARNING"):
    report_link_problems(problems, fake_course(), fatal=False)
  assert "1 link(s) could not be translated in Course 59543" in caplog.text
  assert caplog.text.count("$WIKI_REFERENCE$/pages/x: no page") == 1
