\section{Translating at the command line}

Every command that reads or writes a body offers the same options, added
by one function so that they are spelled and explained the same way
everywhere.
Translation is on by default: the whole point is that a pulled file is
portable without the user remembering a flag, and a push without
symbolic links is unaffected.
[[--no-link-translation]] turns it off, for the user who wants the
numeric links exactly as Canvas has them.
[[--allow-unresolved-links]] exists only on the commands that push, since
the export never fails on a link (\cref{sec:course-links-report}).
<<functions>>=
def add_link_options(parser, push=False):
  """Add the link translation options to `parser`.

  With `push`, also add the option that turns an unresolved link from
  an error into a warning.
  """
  parser.add_argument("--no-link-translation", action="store_true",
    help="Keep links to course objects exactly as they are instead of "
         "translating them between the course-independent symbolic form "
         "and this course's numeric URLs.")
  if push:
    parser.add_argument("--allow-unresolved-links", action="store_true",
      help="Warn and keep the symbolic form when a link cannot be "
           "resolved in the target course, instead of refusing to push.")
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_link_options_are_added():
  import argparse
  view = argparse.ArgumentParser()
  add_link_options(view)
  assert view.parse_args(["--no-link-translation"]).no_link_translation
  assert not hasattr(view.parse_args([]), "allow_unresolved_links")
  push = argparse.ArgumentParser()
  add_link_options(push, push=True)
  args = push.parse_args(["--allow-unresolved-links"])
  assert args.allow_unresolved_links and not args.no_link_translation
@
